ing mall by the train  tracks in Kibera in Kenya this goes on  for kilometers and merchants selling  everything from shoes to use clothing to  vegetables to printed fabric to all  sorts of things and every so often the  train comes through and people get out  of the way this is toy market on the  edge of Kibera this is a huge market  place this is in the rain but it's a  huge market place for produce and  hardware very haphazard as you can see  most of the merchants here are squatters  most of the merchants live in Kibera and  they're doing a brisk business buying  and selling things those green things in  the foreground are mangoes the Kenyan  mangoes are green and they tend to eat  them somewhat hard with with salt or hot  pepper it's kind of interesting it's  good this looks like a picture of  another sewage outflow in key para but  actually you see that thing on the right  is a hotel yes but what they mean by  hotel is not what we mean as hotel a  hotel in Kenya and in India is a  restaurant and there are hotels all over  the squatter communities of Kenya and  India I ate in many hotels in Kenya and  India didn't eaten this one it was a  little too close to the sewage for my  taste but I do have to say and you know  never got sick from eating in the hotels  and the squatter communities  so the food there agreed with me for  whatever reason this hotel is sharing  space right next to it is a butcher shop  and that's rather common in the squad  our communities to particularly in Kenya  where people like what they call niama  Choma or grilled meat so the grilled  meat will be cut up by the butcher and  then served in the hotel and they often  are tandem like this so there's a  tremendous amount of Commerce and it  goes from rather primitive stuff to  rather fancy stuff this is sultan bailey  in istanbul that's a general store their  bizarre there's a furniture store on the  left-hand side of the picture and you  find everything in sultan daily you find  car dealerships you find hardware stores  you find tons of restaurants there's a  post office it's got you don't have to  leave the community to buy almost  anything and as you can see in the the  building with the bazaar in it there's  actually businesses on several of the  floors so it's like an office building  so there's a tremendous amount of  Commerce and all of these communities  and people are making their money albeit  illegally so here's the question how do  you go from this to this or even this  and just as a parenthesis that's not  Hitler Mussolini that's either Turk  although he is pointing out of town  which some people think is so Tom Bailey  the politics of so Tom Daley are weird  it's a squatter community that's  fundamentalist Muslim and they put up  the statue of Ataturk because they want  to be able to show the powers that be  that they are  kamala lists and secular even though  they are religious but they did put him  up pointing out of town so it functions  both ways now my argument is that there  are two things that need to happen to  get development to happen in squalor  communities the first is that people  need a guarantee that they're not going  to be evicted if people think that they  can be evicted tomorrow or with  automatically by government or by  property owners with no recourse  whatsoever they are not going to invest  in their homes now that doesn't mean  that people necessarily need property  rights there's been a couple of years  ago there was a rather popular book that  was published by Hernando de Soto called  the mystery of capital and he argues  that it would be magical if you just  gave all squatters property rights their  communities with blossom but the middle  picture there Hasina people don't have  property rights and they build and the  far right picture Soltan daily people  don't have property rights and they  build so the determinant here is not  whether you have a title deed the  determinant is whether you know that  you're not 