ives turkeys squatters  access to politics the other squatter  communities that I was in don't have  that same access in Brazil people band  together and form residents associations  and then they broke  are their votes to various politicians  for services and things like that but  they don't have the same ability to  organize politically that the squatters  in Turkey do and in Kenya which is the  far left on this slide people don't seem  to have any access to politics at all  partially it's because most decisions  are national decisions in Kenya even  small decisions about the fate of  communities in Nairobi require Acts of  Parliament and it's politics in Kenya is  the zone of the elite sadly the Member  of Parliament who represents this squad  our community of capara is a rich man  who doesn't live there so representative  politics are awful hard in Kenya I would  argue that the squatters need a lot of  organizing to be able to move forward in  Kenya but we can talk about that more  during the question-and-answer period so  I believe that the future of these  communities needs to be determined by  the people on the ground and these are  the people that I see is the civic  leaders of the future the woman in the  middle is Geeta jiwa she is a Mumbai  squatter she lives in a tent on a  highway median she's lived there for six  years she's very outspoken and given the  appropriate empowerment could be a great  leader for her community across the way  in a slightly better location this is  Cherica gundy she and her family also  live in the tent but instead of being on  the median strip there alongside the  access road of the highway so they have  a slightly better location and she's  also very outspoken about her needs this  is her whole family although my friend  Roger would not forgive me if I didn't  point out the guy in the shiny shirt  and the chino pants that are clean he's  also a squatter but he was acting as a  translator for me so he's not part of  her family he's a taxi driver actually  who lived nearby to where I lived this  is Lakshmi she's a pavement dweller  she's a member of an organization called  the Sluman shack dwellers International  and she although for 25 years she's been  living in this little Hut on the  sidewalks of a neighborhood called  nagpada she's been with the members of  SDI she's been to New York she's been to  Cambodia she's been to South Africa  she's been around the world she's been  to more countries than I've been to and  she still lives on the pavement in her  little shack with her parrot outside  this is a Turkish squatter there were  three old ladies who lived in houses  right next to each other and I knew them  only as Nene which means grandma and  she's very proud of her house and very  proud of her garden and she proudly  posed for a number of pictures for me  she's in her 70s and you know lives in  her gadget kondu house all alone I  should note however that expenses got a  little much for her and so she actually  she gave up having electricity because  it was too expensive for her and so  she's living without electricity uh this  is Richard Richard mu gamma and the  Richard is like the great PR man for  Kiera and Kenya he's a roving street  photographer he makes his money by  taking pictures of people he sells the  pictures for fifty cents a snapshot and  that's his business and I was hoping to  have enough money to be able to pay  Richard to take the photos from my book  but it didn't work out that way and he  was very envious of my minolta camera  because he  felt that it was better than his Petry  there and finally this is the man that I  would like to see be the next mayor of  Rio de Janeiro this is a xeno with his  two kids the kid behind the skinny kid  is not his son that's fairly obvious  zezinho is fruit and vegetable merchant  and he's lived in ho senior for 30 years  he started with a pushcart and he roved  the bacos going up and down these mud  and then concrete pathways selling  vegetables from a pushcart and then he  made enough money to open the kiosk and  this is his kiosk along what's called  the estrada da gávea and he's very proud  of his sons and he makes enough money  that he sends his sons to private school  which is a in rio an important thing to  do in if you want your kids to get ahead  the public education system Rios  education system is very backwards on  the primary and secondary level the  public education system stinks you need  to go to private school but if you do  well on the high school exam called the  vestibule are you qualify to go to free  public university which is better than  going to private university so it's it's  a very strange system so in summary we  pull out my notes here there's no mud  hut utopia alright i'm not in favor of  people living with no water no sewers no  sanitation no toilets no electricity no  one in the modern era should have to  live that way but i do argue that  squatters are doing more with less and  more efficiently than any government on  earth in any development on earth they  mix more mortar and lay more brick than  anyone and the key is to let them  harness their own abilities now in if I  were a prosecutor I would be trying to  show  that what is it motive means an  opportunity that you always want to show  that the defendant had well the  squatters have the motive society denies  them a place to live they need to come  to the cities to make money they can't  get a place to live in the city but they  can't get a job so they seize land and  build for themselves they have the means  because they're willing to labor for  themselves to create the home they want  and they do it in a very efficient way  because they're patient when they don't  have money they don't build when they do  have money they build one wall at a time  and many of the huts that I've shown you  in these photos were literally  constructed one wall at a time when  people had the money what they lack and  what they have to fight for and what we  governments around the world can give  them is the opportunity governments need  to meet them halfway provide  infrastructure and materials and a  certain amount of construction expertise  and the squatters will do the rest so  there's a tremendous amount of capacity  out there in squalor communities and if  we can turn ourselves around from  looking as these communities as emblems  of misery to looking at them as  possibilities and if we instead of  blindly without any quid pro quo  forgiving the debt of developing  countries can say to those countries  yeah we'll forgive your debt if you  provide infrastructure to all your  people if you run water pipes into the  areas where people don't have water that  would go a long way to having these  communities develop and flourish and  become better places for people to live  so that's what I wanted to say and I'd  be happy to take all of your questions  thanks  Rob they've secretly been furnishing me  questions all along years okay past some  lasagna and Kevin is going to we got a  lot of questions in a huge crowd I don't  know if it was the squatter cities or  the medieval angle that brought  everybody out here's one from Ryan is  that the Ryan I know now different Ryan  well while property ownership is not an  option in these squatter cities our  residences still considered private  property in bought and sold and rented  accordingly yes that's the short answer  I guess I should give a little  explanation absolutely people buy and  sell and establish value and create a  marketplace even without ownership so in  the sense they are recognizing  possession and buying and selling  possession rather than buying and  selling property and indeed in a place  like Brazil they've actually created  forms which they showed me which are  memorializing the buying and selling of  possession rather than property and this  happens in almost all of the squad our  communities and people also rent with  the same kind of arrangement so among  the informal economic activities does  that include lawyers or out of these  sort out disputes um they don't  generally turn to lawyers it's kind of a  community dispute resolution mechanism  they just sort of argue with out in  Brazil occasionally they will actually  go to the drug lord and ask him to sort  out the disputes and there are occasions  when they work with the government if  they have to but those are rare because  the government doesn't tend to be on  their side so it's more just they sort  of hash things out on their own and  figure it out this may lead to the next  question from anonymous can you say  something about communication within the  squatter villages they have cell phones  phone  thanks okay not a lot they communicate  the way the old-fashioned way they go  talk to each other the most of these  countries the together phone wire is  incredibly bureaucratic and incredibly  expensive they're jumping right from the  wire world to the mobile world but  mobile phones are relatively expensive  to buy a mobile phone new in any of  these countries cost about 100 bucks and  that's just way beyond the means of most  squatters the only place where many many  many squatters have mobile phones is  Turkey where there's a brisk business in  used or stolen mobile phones and many  many of my friends had mobile phones but  otherwise you know people walk and knock  on doors and go talk to each other and  there isn't a lot of technical  capability within the communities just  kind of a practical question from  nameless who probably is going to get on  a plane and try to do what you did  because they want to know how did you do  what you did and mechanically how do you  go from stepping off a plane to living  in a squadron neighborhood how do you  find a vacancy do you have to explain  why you and American want to live there  do you encounter resistance suspicions  what's all that about well it's it's  kind of six degrees of separation you  send out emails and one person refers  you to do another person refers you to  another person refers to you you do  another person what happened with me the  first country I went to was Brazil and I  emailed I got contacts with all the  people that I could get in contact with  a lot of academics and people like that  no one knew anything everyone was  horrified that I was going to live in  the favela because the strange thing is  that most of the academics who study  them never go to them  it's a sad but true so and finally one  person said to me well there's this  crazy American guy who's been hanging  out in Cocina for a while off and on why  don't you email him and I did and he  said sure come on down um and I had the  trust that he was the right guy so in  the first country I had an American who  was completely fluent in Portuguese he's  now a professor of Portuguese at  Oklahoma University and is going to San  Diego State next year but he was down  there and he was establishing an  Educational Foundation which he's still  raising money for and so he brought me  right into the community and he had some  Street credibility there in the other  countries I had to go there I had some  contacts but I had to go there and live  in hotels for about a week or two before  I could make the contacts within the  communities to be able to move in and I  generally worked through church groups  or agencies that had some dealings with  them but I had to get to the squatters  themselves to be able to find places to  live the best story of that was in Kenya  I show up in Kenya and I my contact  there was a missionary for Mary know who  was working as a lawyer giving legal  advice not filing lawsuits but just  giving dispute advice in a church in the  squadra community of capara and I showed  up and she said great nice to have you  here i'm leaving for a two-week vacation  in Tanzania but here's this guy  Nicodemus I trust him with my life and I  found out later that she hardly knew the  guy but I decided all right he's he's  the right guy he seems to have  volunteered to take me on and in fact he  was the right guy  and he became just an incredible friend  and brother and Ombudsman in in that  time there so if you're open enough to  it and and then he worked with me to  negotiate where I was going to live and  that's often how it worked for me so  that's a sort of thumbnail description  so we have so many questions we were  kind of doing a little tag team okay  asking them so here's an inner  utilitarian question from shells it  looks like what if one wanted to  actually build a home there and find a  place to occupy is that difficult to do  and what do you do how do you go about  it do you have to ask well I it's tricky  I I think initially you didn't have to  ask you just went and did it as the  communities have gotten more crowded  there's become more of a procedure that  you need to go through and you generally  have to I mean in Cocina you actually  have to most of the property is  possessed by someone although not  legally owned and you have to arrange  with that person to buy it in in Turkey  there are some squad their communities  where there's still space and you can  just take it but you do have to make  some arrangements with the neighbors  because you're going to have to live  with them and so they're going to have  to be comfortable with who you are and  what you're doing and sadly in Kenya the  squatters I call them squatters but  they're not really squatters the whole  situation in Kenya is just so totally  corrupt the land is not is is  governmentally owned the government  through a sort of corrupt patronage deal  sells temporary occupation licenses to  rich people who erect mud huts and rent  them to the new arrivals so very few of  the 500,000 to a million people living  in Kibera are actually squatters most  them are renters being exploited by rich  people and the reason why rich people do  that is because it's a great investment  you can build a mud hut for next to  nothing and then all the money you earn  is just gravy so it's a weird scenario I  guess the the idea of how you establish  yourself in these communities varies a  little bit from country to country  depending on the how long the squad our  community has been there there are areas  in India where I could set up a tent if  I wanted to and then there are other  squad their communities where I would  have to work out you know whether that  was going to be permitted because the  communities are more dense thnx here's a  question from Mike Ratner okay great  says i'm a member of Engineers Without  Borders a group of volunteers that were  to develop technologies relevant for the  third world and since members abroad  what problems in the squadra cities do  you think may have technological  solutions if any hmm I'm not an engineer  but personally my thought would be it's  not technology that's the issue the  issue really is infrastructure people  build what they can build and when they  have security of tenure they rebuild and  build again and build again as they have  the money to do it so and as you can see  from some of the slides in Turkey they  build pretty well once they develop that  sense that they're going to be able to  stay and that they've had the money over  time I guess the technology that would  be important would be kind of  infrastructure planning if people could  figure out how to bring water pipes or  sewers into communities and thus some of  the money for designing those kinds of  systems might be saved maybe that would  help the municipalities do a better job  at bringing infrastructure in but you  know I've had some of  arguments you know people want you know  wouldn't the internet make a difference  in squatter communities and it's not  that people don't use the internet if  it's around it's just that it's not  their most primary need their most  primary need or the basic necessitie